    "state": "WEB FORM SUBMISSION — Restaurant feedback\n\nSubmitted by: Chiara Lindqvist\nLocation: Juniper Lantern Hotel, dining room\nVisit date: Thursday\n\nI am furious about how my table was handled. This is a complaint about the dinner service, not a request for a free stay or a refund. I booked a table for two at 7:00 p.m. and arrived on time. The host seated us at 7:12. Another couple arrived after us and were seated before we were, although I do not know whether they had a reservation. I am mentioning them because the host later insisted that all tables had been seated in booking order, which plainly was not what I saw.\n\nWe ordered one lentil stew and one grilled trout. My friend ordered the trout, not me. The server brought two stews, then took one away when we corrected the mistake. We waited for the trout, and it came after the rest of the table had finished eating. It was cold in the middle. The server apologized, but nobody offered to replace it. A different guest at the next table complained that her steak was overcooked; that was not our food and should not be written down as my complaint.\n\nThe bill included both the stew and trout. I checked it before paying. I paid the full amount because we had eaten the stew and my friend chose to keep the trout rather than wait again. I am not asking you to return that payment, erase the charge, or give us a voucher. Please do not answer this form by pretending that I demanded money. I want the restaurant manager to explain why an incorrect dish was served and why the correct dish arrived cold and so late.\n\nI also want the manager to contact me personally by email. A generic “we value your feedback” response is not an explanation. If the restaurant wants to offer something voluntarily, that is its choice, but I am not requesting compensation and will not accept an offer as a substitute for answering what happened. The host’s seating claim should be checked separately: it is not the main reason I am writing, but it made an already irritating evening worse.\n\nFor clarity, the party seated ahead of us had a blue scarf and ordered steak. The other party near the window had a stroller and asked for a high chair. Neither detail concerns our order. Our table was number 22, and the receipt number ends in 681. The server called my friend “the trout guest” when checking the plate, so the kitchen had the information it needed. The ticket printout may show a second stew because that is what was first entered, but it does not mean we asked for two stews.\n\nI understand that restaurants get busy. That does not excuse serving food that was ordered incorrectly, then making a guest wait for the replacement dish until everyone else had finished. Please review the kitchen ticket and speak to the person who served our table. I would like a reply stating what you found, not a copy of your general service policy. I do not need another survey link, and I do not want a reservation offer for another night. A direct explanation from the manager is what I am requesting.\n\nI am submitting this form because the server said the manager might not see a verbal complaint. Please confirm that this has reached the manager. I am angry, but I have tried to make the facts clear: we were two guests, one trout was ordered, it came late and cold, and the initial duplicate stew was not what we ordered. Please address those points without mixing in the other guests’ steak, stroller, or seating details.",
