ncluded here because the dashboard exports a small comparison panel.\n\n13:09:50 — Security scan finished. It listed 6 informational notices and 0 blocking findings. The pipeline rule treats informational notices as non-blocking. The notices are not additional failed tests.\n\n13:10:03 — Artifact packaging started. It wrote route-planner.tar and checksum.txt. The artifact was present in the workspace and had size 18.4 MB. The upload step did not begin because the required-check gate was evaluated first.\n\n13:10:12 — Gate evaluation: compile passed; unit check failed; integration check failed; security scan passed. Overall status: failed. No artifact was published. The service account credentials were valid, and there was no network error. The log line “upload queue: 0” means nothing was placed in the upload queue, not that a zero-byte artifact was created.\n\n13:12:33 — Notification sent to the project channel. Message: “Please do not manually publish this package. Fix the assertions and run the pipeline again.” A later comment asks whether the green count on the card might mean most checks passed. The release coordinator replies that the gate requires every check to pass, not a majority.\n\n13:15:09 — A diagnostic command printed 126 + 37 successful checks across the two test suites. This sum is useful for reporting, but it does not change the individual suite statuses. The compact report also shows 4 + 3 failing tests. No retry was performed after the gate failed.\n\nEnd of excerpt. There is no later successful run in this exported record. The release label remained pending, and the deployment dashboard continued to show the prior stable version.",
