    "state": "Product review submitted to the Copperleaf Health developer-tools marketplace by Wanjiru Sorensen\n\nProduct reviewed: Patchlight, a small dashboard for watching automated checks in a software project. Review title: “A tidy window, with one important catch.” The review was left after several weeks of use by a small team maintaining an appointment-booking application. The writer says they are describing their own setup and not giving a general guarantee for every company.\n\nWe installed Patchlight because the existing build page was noisy, and the team wanted one place to see the check history. Installation was uncomplicated. The browser panel appeared after we added the project token and selected our main repository. It does not replace the command line or the build service. It reads status information that the service already provides, then organizes it in a calmer way.\n\nIn the first week, the panel showed the latest run and let me open the detail view. The detail view was useful because it kept the check names beside their results. We had compile, unit, and packaging checks. The panel did not invent extra checks, which is important when people are looking at a release under pressure. The display uses small colored marks, and the labels are also written out, so the screen is understandable even when colors are hard to distinguish.\n\nI counted the time needed to bring one ordinary project into the panel. From opening the settings to seeing the first imported status, it took about 12 minutes. A colleague separately timed a larger project at 19 minutes, but that project had an additional access approval. Do not combine those two setup observations as though they were consecutive stages of one setup; they were separate projects. The product page says setup is “usually under fifteen minutes.” My own observation fits that statement, while the second example does not. I would not make a broad claim from only these two observations.\n\nFor review, we looked at 25 recent build records. Seventeen finished successfully, five stopped because a required check failed, and three were cancelled by a person before completion. The panel shows the cancellation separately, which is better than making it look like a successful run. The 17 successful records are the only ones I mean when I say that the team had green results. In a separate trial, we counted 8 checks within one particular run; that count is not the number of successful build records.\n\nThe feature I use most is the short comparison between the current run and its previous run. It showed that the packaging stage had become slower, even though the source changes were small. A teammate initially thought the dashboard was reporting a failed release because the comparison used an orange marker. The written status clarified that the build had completed and the marker meant “slower than its previous result,” not “failed.”\n\nThere are limitations. The panel refresh is not instant. During one afternoon, a finished run appeared after roughly four minutes. Another update appeared after roughly six minutes. Those were two different refresh waits, not a stated average or an advertised maximum. The product should explain that delay more clearly. It also could make the history export easier to find. The export did work when we found it, and the file included the run status and the time.\n\nSupport answered my question about a missing repository permission the next morning. The answer was clear, although it would be useful to show that permission requirement during setup. I have not tested the product with a very large organization, a self-hosted server, or a disconnected network, so I cannot comment on those situations. The review is not a complaint about data loss: we did not lose a build record. My main suggestion is better explanation of refresh timing and access permissions. For our small project, Patchlight made the history easier to read, and I continue to use it.",
