    "state": "Bluefin Outfitters — development services agreement, selected clauses and amendment record\n\nDocument reference: BO-DEV-27. This excerpt concerns the internal build and test service used by the Bluefin Outfitters engineering group. It is written as a working contract copy, not as a customer support message. The parties agree that the defined terms below apply to the clauses in this excerpt unless an amendment expressly changes them.\n\nOriginal clause 2.1, Service allowance: The provider will make available up to 500 build minutes in each calendar month for the covered project. Minutes are counted from the start of a build job until that job ends. Time spent waiting in the queue before a job starts is not counted. The allowance is a monthly cap, not a promise that the team will use all of it. Unused minutes do not roll into the following month.\n\nOriginal clause 2.2, Additional usage: If the covered project uses more than the monthly allowance, the customer may be charged for excess usage at the rate stated in the order form. The provider must show the recorded usage before issuing an excess-usage invoice. A usage estimate is not itself an invoice, and an alert does not establish that the allowance has been exceeded.\n\nOriginal clause 3.1, Required checks: A release package may be designated ready only if every required check configured for that release has a passing result. A skipped check is not a passing result. A cancelled check is also not a passing result. The project owner may change the list of required checks, but any such change must be recorded in the project settings before the relevant build begins.\n\nOriginal clause 3.2, Test count: The monthly report may state both the number of successful test cases and the number of unsuccessful test cases. Those counts describe test cases, not build jobs. One build job can run many test cases. The provider shall not represent a successful test-case total as a successful-build total.\n\nOriginal clause 4.1, Service response: A support response is due within two business days after a valid request is received. The receipt date is excluded from the count. Saturday, Sunday, and the listed company closure day are not business days. A response sent earlier than the deadline satisfies the response time. This clause concerns the provider’s response, not the resolution of the underlying issue.\n\nAmendment A, signed by both parties on 6 May: For the covered project only, the monthly build-minute allowance in clause 2.1 is changed from 500 minutes to 620 minutes. All other wording in clauses 2.1 and 2.2 remains unchanged. Amendment A does not change the price rate in the order form, the method for counting job time, or the rule that unused minutes do not roll forward.\n\nAmendment B, signed on 9 May: For releases begun on or after 1 June, the team adds a security scan to its required checks. The scan is not required for builds begun before that date. The amendment does not state that informational scan notices constitute failures; the general configured-check result remains the result recorded by the service. This document does not identify a build date for any particular release unless that date is separately stated in a report.\n\nUsage report attached for June: Recorded job time was 581 minutes. Queue waiting added 46 minutes but is excluded under clause 2.1. The dashboard displayed an estimate of 627 minutes by combining job time and waiting time. The provider’s detailed accounting field shows 581 billable build minutes. A note in the project channel says, “Looks like we used 627 minutes, so prepare an overage,” but that note is a preliminary reading of the dashboard estimate, not a change to the agreement.\n\nBuild report attached for 12 June: A release job completed with compile passing, unit tests passing, and security scan passing. The test report lists 240 successful cases and 6 unsuccessful cases. The job itself is one completed build job. A separate row from 30 May lists 2 failed security checks, but that older row is from before Amendment B took effect and belongs to a different build.\n\nSupport record: The provider received a valid request on Monday, 15 June. It sent a response on Tuesday, 16 June. There was no company closure day in that interval. The response acknowledged the request and gave a troubleshooting link; the contract does not require the issue to be fully fixed by the response deadline.",
