    "state": "Northwind Outfitters — product review of TernBuild developer workflow monitor, written by Esi Rahman\n\nI tried TernBuild with our sample application because the old status screen is difficult for newer people in the team. I do not maintain the company’s production systems, and this review is only about the test workspace I was given. The setup page explains how to connect a repository, choose a branch, and select which checks should appear. It is written in simple language, though some button labels are different from the words used in the help article.\n\nThe monitor gave me a single page with the status of a build, test activity, and an artifact link. The first time I opened it, the artifact link was gray. I thought this meant the artifact was missing, but the detail panel said it had been created and was waiting for an authorized reviewer. The monitor does not publish a package by itself. It displays the state reported by the build service. That difference should be made more visible because a gray link looks like a problem to someone who has not used the tool before.\n\nI used the test workspace for four runs. In run A, the compile check and test check both passed. In run B, compile passed but the tests failed. In run C, the job was cancelled by a team member before the compile check finished. In run D, both checks passed, but the artifact had not yet been approved for release. These are four build runs, not four test cases. The monitor’s overview marks A and D as completed successfully at the check level, B as failed, and C as cancelled. D is not yet released, which is a separate status.\n\nThe service report for run A lists 31 passing test cases and 2 failing test cases. A summary tile says “31 good,” and a note beside the tile says that this count should not be read as the total number of cases. Run B lists 27 passing cases and 5 failing cases. In another panel, the total number of configured checks is 6; that number counts check categories, not individual test cases. I found it helpful to keep those figures separate when discussing whether a run was ready.\n\nThe history panel was easy to filter. I selected the four runs and exported a small file. In the file, the two runs whose checks passed are listed as “completed,” while the failed and cancelled runs have separate labels. This is reasonable, but the screen does not say that cancellation differs from failure until you open the legend. I would prefer the explanation to be visible next to the labels.\n\nI also measured one page load on two different days. On the first day, it took around 3 seconds; on the second, it took around 5 seconds. This is not a controlled performance test, and I did not measure a mean. The marketing page says “typically under 10 seconds,” which is consistent with both of my observations, but I have not tried the tool with a large history or a slow connection. The number of team members, 12, is shown in the workspace header and does not mean that 12 people were simultaneously using the monitor.\n\nThere is a small issue with how approval is shown. The artifact appeared available after about 2 minutes on one run and about 4 minutes on another, but I did not determine whether the wait was caused by the monitor or by the build service. These are two separate observations, not a guaranteed release delay. The useful thing is that the status detail tells you which service event was last received. I did not see any evidence that a test result was altered or hidden.\n\nOverall, I would recommend a clearer explanation of the approval state and more visible definitions for the labels. For a small team that mainly wants to read recent check results, the page is less crowded than our previous tool. I cannot say whether it is suitable for large installations because I did not try one. The review is about readability, not about the reliability of an entire software delivery process.",
