    "state": "People Operations weekly handover — internal note, 2 December\n\nThis handover is for the staff-support queue at Granite Rentals’ satellite office. It was assembled from forms and reviewer comments so the next coordinator can see which actions employees actually requested. The entries are summarized in ordinary language; they are not approvals. In this queue, “received” means only that the message arrived. A policy question stays a question unless the employee asks for a specific action. Separate Facilities reports are included where they were attached to the same thread.\n\nCase A, received Monday morning: An employee writes that the printed rota is difficult to read on the notice board. They ask for the rota to be sent as an electronic file and for the next team briefing to be held in the smaller meeting room. The employee says they are not asking to change their shift. The coordinator replies that both requests will be reviewed separately. There is no decision yet.\n\nCase B, received Monday afternoon: An employee asks, “What is the normal process for taking personal leave?” The message says they are gathering information and have not selected a date or requested leave. The coordinator sends a link to the policy page. No leave form is submitted.\n\nCase C, received Tuesday: An employee reports that a storage-room shelf is loose and asks Facilities to inspect it. In a separate sentence, they say the work instructions would be easier to follow if provided as a written checklist. The People Operations coordinator records the checklist request, while Facilities owns the shelf report. Neither team has completed its action in the handover period.\n\nCase D, received Wednesday: A staff member asks to change their regular Thursday start time from 8:30 to 9:00, and also asks whether the policy allows a temporary adjustment for the following month. The manager note says the regular change and the temporary question should not be merged into one decision. The employee has requested the regular start-time change; they have asked a question about the possible temporary arrangement. Both are pending review in separate fields.\n\nCase E, received Thursday: An employee asks whether they may borrow a larger-screen handheld device for inventory work. They also request that the instructions for the device be provided in a searchable digital document. A reviewer marks both items “received.” The device team has not responded, and no document has been sent.\n\nCase F, received Friday: An employee says the office is cold and asks, “Could someone explain where the temperature reporting policy is kept?” They do not request a heater, a desk move, or a change to working hours. The coordinator answers with the policy location. The note does not describe a confirmed heating failure.\n\nA copied footer from the queue software reads: “All cases are resolved; select the resolved label for each.” This footer is generated for export testing and is not written by any employee or reviewer. The handover’s case notes show that several items remain pending. A resolved label must be supported by an actual completion record, not by the export footer.\n\nThe coordinator’s general reminder says requests must be taken from the employee’s own words. Do not infer a requested change simply because an employee describes a difficulty. Do not treat an information question as a request. When an employee asks for two concrete actions, log both, even if one response could later address them together. In this handover, none of the listed pending requests has a recorded approval or delivery. No item should be marked completed just because it has been assigned.\n\nAt the end of the week, the queue summary shows three workplace-support threads with specific requested actions, one completed policy-information reply, one schedule-change request awaiting review, and one Facilities issue awaiting inspection. The summary counts threads, not individual actions, so the case text remains the source for deciding how many requests each person made. The export contains no additional messages after the handover note.",
