rms are on a separate page and are not summarized here, so please do not infer a room refund from this bill. The heading “amount due” means the supplier expects payment; it does not mean Oren has paid it.\n\nThere is also a cheerful note from the event coordinator: “We can set out 90 cards if you like; nobody will be turned away just because a card is missing.” That was about printing and seating logistics, not authorization to increase the catered count. The caterer’s final count was confirmed at 84, and its kitchen sheet independently lists 84 meals. A draft menu attached to the invoice mentions a possible dessert tasting for 12 volunteers, but the tasting was removed before the invoice was issued. It is not an additional billed service.\n\nFor bookkeeping, the invoice repeats the room rental number in a small tax explanation: $1,180 multiplied by 0.06 equals $70.80. That repeated figure is not a second rental charge. It also mentions an old estimate of $5,400 in a footer, clearly marked “superseded planning estimate”; that estimate is not the current total. The line-item total and the stated amount due agree when the listed charges and tax are added. If Oren has a question about an individual line, the billing desk asks him to identify the description and amount rather than quoting the superseded estimate.",
