ep the lamps on the order for now.” That exchange concerned only the $240 equipment hold. It did not cancel the event, request a return of money, or change the $600 security deposit.\n\nThe last reply, sent May 12 at 9:14 a.m., is from Chiara to our booking desk: “Please return the $600 security deposit to the card ending 1842. I am keeping the event and the lamps; I only want the refundable security deposit back because our venue contract has now waived it.” The message from the venue confirming that waiver is included below. It says the venue will not require a security deposit for this booking. Chiara’s request is therefore specifically for the $600 security deposit, not the planning fee, equipment hold, vendor quote, or the whole booking. Our desk has not yet processed the requested return.\n\nFor clarity, “return the deposit” in the earlier subject line was copied forward automatically and should not be read as a request from the booking desk. Chiara wrote the May 12 message. The booking desk’s response later that morning was: “Received; we will review the ledger.” That acknowledgment is not a denial, approval, or payment confirmation. The ledger still lists the $600 as held, the $180 planning fee as earned, and the $240 equipment hold pending equipment check-in.\n\nThe attached venue note also mentions a $900 damage bond used for a different pavilion booking last summer. That booking belonged to another client and is not part of Harborlight. It appears here because the venue reused a template. Likewise, a note about a possible $150 cleaning credit was a hypothetical estimate in an earlier planning conversation; no credit was issued. Please use the current Harborlight amounts and the May 12 request rather than those unrelated figures.",
