    "state": "Terms excerpt sent with the Fernbank Makers’ Supper booking confirmation\n\nHello Priya, and welcome to the mildly sprawling terms sheet. We know event terms are not exactly party conversation, but it is easier to explain the odd corners now than while everyone is carrying centerpieces. This excerpt applies to the Fernbank Makers’ Supper booked at the Glasshouse on August 16. The organizer named in the booking is Priya Kaur. The policy below is the version attached to her confirmation; a sample policy from a different venue is also reproduced at the end for comparison and does not govern this booking.\n\nCANCELLATION AND REFUND RULES\n\n1. A written cancellation request received at least 21 calendar days before the event qualifies for a return of the room deposit, less a $40 administration charge. The event date itself is not counted as a full day before the event. For this booking, the room deposit was $500. If this rule applies, the amount returned is $460. Catering payments, rental charges for equipment already delivered, and the $90 planning fee are governed by their separate clauses.\n\n2. A request received fewer than 21 calendar days before the event does not qualify for a return of the room deposit. A request can be made by email to the booking desk. A question about whether cancellation might be necessary is not a cancellation request. A conditional statement such as “we may cancel if the weather is bad” does not cancel an event.\n\n3. Exception: if the venue itself closes the Glasshouse for a safety issue, the room deposit is returned in full, regardless of how many days remain. A forecast of rain, a client’s concern about rain, or a closure at a different property is not a venue closure under this exception. This clause overrides the standard 21-day calculation only when the Glasshouse is actually closed by the venue for safety.\n\n4. The attendance guarantee is due 10 calendar days before the event. That number governs the final catering count, not the cancellation deadline. A draft schedule elsewhere may mention 14 days for an older package, but this booking uses the 10-day rule. The deposit rule remains 21 calendar days.\n\nBOOKING LOG AND ATTACHED CORRESPONDENCE\n\nMay 1: Priya pays the $500 room deposit and $90 planning fee. The receipt also contains a $300 estimate for optional chair rentals; no chair rental payment is recorded. A note says the caterer’s $1,240 proposal is not yet accepted.\n\nJuly 20: Priya emails, “Just checking: if the Glasshouse closes for a safety issue, the deposit comes back, right? We are not cancelling. The forecast says possible showers, but the venue has not closed.” The booking desk replies that the exception requires an actual venue closure. This is a question, not a cancellation.\n\nJuly 25: A message from another customer, forwarded accidentally into the planning folder, says, “Please cancel my August 12 garden lunch.” That customer was discussing the Orchard Room, a different property, and the message was not written by Priya. It does not alter this booking.\n\nJuly 26: The Glasshouse manager confirms: “We are open as scheduled. We have not closed the venue for a safety issue. The forecast is unsettled, but the building remains available.” A neighboring garden venue postponed an outdoor workshop that week; its decision has no effect on the Glasshouse.\n\nAugust 1: Priya emails the booking desk: “Please cancel my August 16 Glasshouse booking. I understand this is too late for the standard room-deposit return; I am asking you to cancel anyway. The forecast looks lovely now, so this is not about weather.” The booking desk acknowledges receipt. No later message withdraws this written cancellation, and the venue does not close.\n\nThe file also contains a comparison box copied from the Marigold Pavilion sample: its own rules use a 14-day cutoff and a $25 administration charge. That sample is for a different venue and client. Do not substitute its deadline or fee for the Fernbank terms above. The current event date, request date, actual closure status, and applicable policy are all recorded in this excerpt.",
