ogram. A spare mixer was present in the equipment room but was never substituted.\n\nThe count sheet signed at close lists 72 lunches served, 80 programs placed, and four microphones returned. The organizer asked for 72 meals, not 80; 80 refers to printed programs. There were 76 names on the registration export, but four registrants had marked “attending virtually.” The final catering order stayed at 72. Another spreadsheet contains a note “maybe add 8 lunches,” but it was never approved and does not change the signed count.\n\nAfter the event, Sven wrote: “Please apply the $350 console credit; the console was missing at doors-open.” The supplier replied with the timeline and checklist above and denied that the credit condition was met. Sven then clarified that he had mistaken the 5:22 reboot for the doors-open status; he did not withdraw his request, but the facts in the event log establish when the console was ready. The supplier has not issued the credit. The term requires failure to provide the console by 5:30, not merely a reboot before the sound check.\n\nThe settlement summary charges the $2,400 base fee and the three approved add-ons: $1,296 for lunches, $160 for programs, and $100 for microphones. These sum to $3,956. It shows no console charge and no console credit. An internal note mentions a $300 courtesy credit discussed for a future booking, but that is not part of this settlement. Another note says the caterer might charge $19 per lunch if the count exceeded 75; the final count was 72, so that conditional rate was not triggered. Please use the signed order and close-out record, not the draft counts or the future-booking note.",
