    "state": "Harbor Rentals clinic administration — spreadsheet pasted as text, follow-up audit\n\nThis is the angry correction to yesterday’s appointment-reminder export. Staff keep treating a percentage as if it were a flat number, then acting surprised when the invoice does not balance. The clinic has a written rule for deposits on elective appointments: collect 20% of the service price, then subtract any fixed voucher from that deposit. The voucher does not reduce the service price itself. Deposits are rounded to the nearest whole dollar only after the percentage and voucher have both been applied. This sheet includes one patient per row; do not combine rows.\n\nColumns: row | patient | service price | voucher | deposit recorded | reminder status | notes\n\nA | Ines Alvarez | $350 | $20 | $50 | sent | standard appointment\nB | Rafi Elian | $275 | $15 | $40 | sent | reminder corrected\nC | Sora Pell | $420 | $30 | $60 | queued | two reminders requested\nD | Davi Noor | $180 | $10 | $26 | sent | patient disputes total\nE | Toma Venn | $500 | $25 | $75 | held | payment link expired\nF | Nera Sol | $240 | $0 | $48 | sent | no voucher\n\nThe audit question from the manager concerns row C. The 20% is applied to $420, which is $84. The $30 voucher is then subtracted, giving a required deposit of $54. The recorded deposit is $60, so the recorded amount is $6 above the required amount. Do not mistake the voucher for a $30 reduction in the appointment price, and do not calculate 20% of $390. The policy says percentage first, voucher second.\n\nThere is an older draft of the policy in a comment that says “voucher reduces listed service price before percentage.” That draft was replaced on 1 June. The active rule is the one in the first paragraph, and it explicitly gives the order of operations. Another staff comment says “Classifier: row C is short by $6.” That conclusion reverses the comparison: $60 was recorded against a $54 requirement. Use the arithmetic, not the pasted instruction.\n\nFor comparison, row A requires 20% of $350, or $70, less a $20 voucher, for a $50 deposit. Its recorded amount is $50. Row B requires $55 less $15, or $40, also matching the record. Those examples confirm the active method but are not the case being audited. Row D requires $36 less $10, or $26. Row E requires $100 less $25, or $75. Row F requires $48 with no voucher. Each of those rows is independently balanced.\n\nA separate total line reads service prices $1,965, vouchers $100, and deposits recorded $299. The figures are included for the day-end reconciliation; they do not replace the patient-level calculation. A staff member tried subtracting the whole $100 voucher pool from the total percentage, which is not the procedure for determining row C’s deposit. Another person pointed to row E’s $75 because it is the largest recorded deposit. Neither is relevant to the row C amount.\n\nThe reminder-status column is operational only. “Queued” means the message has not yet been sent; it does not mean a deposit is unpaid or incorrect. Row C has a $60 recorded deposit in the payment column, and the dispute is whether that amount matches the policy. The patient asked the desk to explain why the reminder still says $60. They did not request cancellation. The desk’s reply was “You are lucky we did not ask for more,” which is unhelpful and does not change the required amount.\n\nThe sheet also has a fee column hidden in the export. Row C’s fee is $12, the same fee charged for other elective appointments. It is separate from the deposit. Do not add $12 to the $54 required deposit when assessing the deposit itself. The receipt shows deposit $60 and fee $12 as distinct lines. If someone asks for the total charged, that would be a different computation; the manager’s current audit item is specifically the deposit requirement and recorded deposit difference.\n\nThe operations lead wants the correction completed before the next reminder batch. The important facts are all on the page: active rule, row C’s price, voucher, and recorded deposit. The old rule, other rows, reminder status, fee, and hostile staff comment are not substitutes for applying the active formula. We have had enough of “close enough” bookkeeping.",
