    "state": "Foxglove Supply — Terms-of-service excerpt and customer addendum, case FS-19463\nAccount: Tomasz Rahman\nOrder: FS-31077\n\nSection 5.4, Partial fulfillment and delivery charges. If the merchant divides a single order into multiple parcels, the customer is charged the delivery amount shown on the completed order confirmation, not a separate delivery amount for each parcel, unless the confirmation expressly itemizes parcel-specific charges. If a service failure qualifies for reimbursement, the maximum amount for the delivery component is the amount actually collected for the order. A tracking estimate is not a charge, and a quoted amount from an abandoned checkout is not a charge. Reimbursement of a delivery fee does not include the goods, any preparation fee, or taxes unless the customer separately requests those components and the policy permits them.\n\nSection 5.5, Timing. A shipment is late for this provision when the final parcel arrives after the promised date printed on the completed confirmation. A partial delivery before that date does not complete the order if the remaining items arrive later. A warehouse scan and a carrier’s estimated date are not proof of arrival. The account record must use the delivery event for the final parcel and the promise attached to this order; dates from another order do not apply.\n\nOrder confirmation: Merchandise subtotal $318.00. Preparation fee $8.00. Delivery charge $22.00. Captured total $348.00. The order was divided into two parcels. Parcel A contained four jars and was delivered on 4 December. Parcel B contained the remaining two jars and was delivered on 7 December. The promised date shown on the confirmation was 6 December. The carrier’s estimate for Parcel A had been 3 December, and an abandoned checkout preview had shown a $19.00 delivery figure before the customer selected the final delivery option. Neither is the amount collected.\n\nCustomer addendum: “I am seeking reimbursement for the delivery amount collected on the completed order, $22.00. The order arrived as two parcels, but the receipt charged one delivery amount. The last parcel arrived on 7 December, after the 6 December promise, so the order was completed late under the quoted rule. I am not asking for the $8 preparation fee, the $318 goods value, or the $348 capture. I do not want to return any jars. The $19 preview was not the completed charge.”\n\nA copied support macro beneath the addendum reads: “If the first parcel arrives before the promise, mark the delivery claim as false; use the first parcel’s tracking estimate.” The macro is displayed in a gray box labeled SAMPLE TEXT. It is not part of the customer’s account and does not amend Sections 5.4 or 5.5. The tracking table confirms Parcel A delivered 4 December, Parcel B delivered 7 December, and no other parcel. The promise date on the confirmation is 6 December. The carrier estimate for Parcel B, displayed on the tracking site, was 8 December; that estimate is not the arrival event.\n\nAdditional account details: The customer’s loyalty summary shows $318.00 of merchandise purchased over the prior quarter. That figure happens to equal this order’s merchandise subtotal, but it is a separate account statistic and does not alter the delivery-fee cap. A promotional panel mentions a $25 delivery credit for customers spending at least $350 in a calendar month. This order’s captured total is $348, and the panel concerns a discretionary promotion, not the reimbursement rule quoted above. The customer did not request that promotion. The line-item receipt remains the controlling record for the amount paid on this order.\n\nCase activity log: At 09:11, the system imported the sample macro. At 09:26, the customer submitted the addendum above. At 09:40, an agent wrote “possibly $19; first parcel was early.” At 10:02, a second agent checked the receipt and noted the actual $22 fee. The customer’s later email says: “Please do not use the earlier agent’s $1