    "state": "Typed-up note found folded inside the attendance binder, apparently written quickly during the afternoon office rush. The handwriting was described as “chatty and slanted, with arrows everywhere.” The typist kept the repetitions because the repetitions seem to show what the writer meant, rather than polishing it into something more official. Header on the scrap: “Nadia Kaur — please put this in my file, not on the public board.”\n\nOkay, so the course is Garden Maps for Beginners, and I am writing this before I get distracted by the hallway printer again. I have the regular class on 4 May and then the optional drawing session on 11 May. The confirmation sheet says the drawing session is optional, but I paid for the whole bundle because it was cheaper than buying each part separately. There is also the little plant-label kit, which I picked up at the counter already. I know people keep saying the kit is “included,” but on my receipt it is a separate line, and I have it in my tote bag now.\n\nThe office person told me I could ask about changing things, and I did ask. That is not the same as saying I am changing everything, I know. The actual thing I want is this: please take me out of the regular class and the optional drawing session. Both, yes, those two class parts. I don't want either of them kept active. I am not asking to be taken out of the plant-label kit because I already collected it, and I am not asking to undo the payment for that kit in this note. I am also not telling the office to remove my name from the garden-club mailing list. That list is a different thing and I still like the monthly notices.\n\nI wrote “maybe I should quit the garden club too” in the margin, but that was me talking to myself while trying to remember the meeting time. It is not a request. Please do not treat the margin as if it were an instruction. If I really wanted to leave the club, I would write it clearly on a separate form. The club is not the same as the course