t out.\n\nThe attendance sheet has a tick beside my name for the orientation demonstration. That demonstration already happened on 18 April, before my note. It was a free orientation, not one of the paid bundle sections. I attended it, and I am not requesting that anybody erase the attendance mark. It should not be confused with the regular class just because both entries use the word “class” in the spreadsheet. The course page also mentions a garden-club open house in June; I have not signed up for that and am not cancelling it.\n\nI asked the receptionist whether I could keep the drawing handout even if I stop attending. She said she would check. Asking about the handout does not mean I want to stay enrolled in drawing. The two things can be separate. I am asking to withdraw from the two paid course sections, while asking a question about a handout. If that is not possible, please let me know, but the request is still to withdraw from both sections. I am not saying “if the instructor changes the date, I might withdraw”; I am saying please record the withdrawal now.\n\nThe little list at the bottom is the clearest bit, I think: “regular class — out; drawing session — out; plant-label kit — already collected, leave alone; garden club — stay on notices.” I wrote “out” because I was in a hurry, but the sentence above says what that means. I did not write “out of the whole cooperative.” I will still be taking the watercolor class in autumn if my schedule behaves. There is no watercolor registration to change today, though.\n\nIf someone needs to know whether the two sections count as two separate actions, yes, they do. They are separate lines on the confirmation sheet and both have their own dates. If the office system has only one overall course switch, please don't use it without checking, because I want the account to show what I actually said. The bundle can be treated however the published rules require for money, but the requested sections are not mysterious: reg