s confirmed, so terminology lab remains scheduled. Someone wrote “maybe postpone” before the confirmation came through. That note is old. The final review room has been booked from 1:00 to 2:30 p.m.; a 3:00 p.m. slot appears in an adjacent room's booking and belongs to the conversation club. Do not mix those entries up. Likewise, the language-club announcement uses the word “translation” in an example sentence, but it is not part of this course's registration.\n\nThe request ledger distinguishes “withdrawal requested” from “fee adjustment approved.” Rosa's three component requests are recorded, but finance has not yet applied an adjustment. The terms require review based on the timing of the request and whether any materials are returned. There is no need to infer a refund from her question about credits. A later note saying “we can probably give something back” is the clerk's informal comment, not an approved outcome. If finance grants a credit, that will be a separate ledger event.\n\nFor completeness, another message in the mailbox is from the instructor to the Registrar: “Please withdraw Rosa from the remaining sessions if she agrees.” That sentence is conditional and written by the instructor; on its own it is neither a student request nor proof of agreement. The later message from Rosa provides her own request and names three components. In the meeting summary, please report the student request accurately rather than copying the instructor's broader phrase “remaining sessions,” since one completed component is specifically to be retained on her account.\n\nI checked whether she had submitted a separate whole-course form. There is no such form. The form portal shows three selections and one unselected box, consistent with her email. The unselected box is intake seminar. The completion marker says “attended,” and an attendance correction from 3 November confirms that she was present. That attendance marker is not itself a request, but it matches her written inst