    "state": "Northwind Analytics — chatty policy page for notices and invoice adjustments\n\nHello, small-business contract people. This page is the slightly less sleepy summary of the notice and invoice-adjustment schedule between Northwind Analytics and Xiu Rahman. It applies to the analytics support agreement signed March 1, 2026. The service year runs March 1 through February 28, 2027, and the annual base fee is $12,000, billed as twelve equal monthly installments of $1,000. The separately priced data-export package costs $240 per year if selected. The agreement's signed order page says Xiu selected the package, and the first invoice records it as paid. This page is meant to clarify the arithmetic, not quietly amend the signed order.\n\nNotices to terminate at renewal must be received at least 30 calendar days before the service year ends. Count the day after receipt as day one when measuring the interval to the end date. The portal receipt says Xiu submitted the notice on January 29, 2027. February 1 through February 28 are 28 calendar days, and January 30 and 31 add two more, for 30 days after the notice date. Thus January 29 is exactly at the 30-day threshold. The service desk notes that an email sent on January 30 would have only 29 days by this convention, despite being “about a month ahead” in casual conversation.\n\nA renewal termination notice means an unambiguous present instruction to stop the agreement at its renewal date, rather than a question about how termination works or a statement about what might happen later. Is Xiu's portal message such a notice? The message says: “Please do not renew my Northwind Analytics agreement after February 28. This is my notice under the 30-day clause. I am not cancelling current access early; please let the existing term run to its end.” That is the complete operative message. The surrounding help chat includes a line from a different account saying, “If we lose another report, we will cancel,” but that line is no