olicy lines were approved earlier in the meeting. Likewise, the comment “what a wonderfully fast 26-minute build” was dry sarcasm; it does not mean the build passed the 25-minute cutoff. The build at 24:59 did pass the duration limit, regardless of the red unit-test icon beside it.\n\nHere is the complete relevant table as we read it aloud: candidate build duration 25:40; docs build duration 24:59; nightly image duration 26:10; artifact exporter transfer 1.8 GB; container publisher transfer 1.6 GB; source-map uploader transfer 1.5 GB; contract suite failures 7 of 30; schema suite failures 6 of 30; smoke suite failures 5 of 30. The storage tile and rerun detail are contextual only, except that the rerun confirms the schema failure count rather than creating a new suite. There are other red signals in the dashboard, including a stale branch and a missing label, but neither is one of the measured conditions in these questions.\n\nWe agreed to report the qualifying records, not the number of warning cards. A warning card can describe a whole job and list multiple measurements, so do not collapse all evidence into one result. One last thing from my notes: the release coordinator said “three groups are bad,” but was counting the number of dashboard panels, not giving an authoritative count of the individual threshold violations. The exact per-measurement policy above is the deciding evidence.",
