lete. A resident’s report about a different fixture is handled separately. In the current maintenance log, the west stairwell light is marked “out—work order submitted; appointment pending.” The third-floor landing light is marked “operating.” Do not treat the completed north entrance bulb as evidence that all building lights have been checked or repaired.\n\nQuestion: Can a technician enter an apartment without contacting the resident?\nAnswer: For the repair requests summarized in this FAQ, the office asks the technician to contact the resident and agree on a time. A routine inspection of the lobby does not authorize entry into a private apartment. The appointment status and any entry arrangement are recorded on the individual work order.\n\nQuestion: Which older jobs are still open?\nAnswer: The archive contains a completed second-floor tap repair from January and a completed lobby door adjustment from March. These are retained for reference and should not be entered as present resident complaints. The current log separately lists a slow kitchen tap reported by a resident in unit 2C; an appointment has not yet been arranged. A reference to another apartment’s completed tap job does not change the status of that current report.\n\nQuestion: How should staff summarize a resident’s message?\nAnswer: Use the resident’s own stated issue and request. Do not infer a request for money from a repair complaint, or infer damage to plants from a supply delivery. When several records use similar words, confirm the location and status before creating a summary. The information above is intended to help residents and staff distinguish current reports from completed work, general policy, and unrelated archive entries. For corrections to the FAQ itself, send a note to the property office; that correction process does not open a maintenance job unless the message also reports a current physical problem.",
