    "state": "Meridian Analytics — incident review meeting notes\nMeeting date: 14 June. Facilitator: Bao Castillo. Attendees: product operations, payments reliability, customer support, and account management. These notes are intended to preserve the decisions and evidence discussed, not to replace the incident record. The group agreed to use the incident channel’s final disposition when reporting whether a particular customer’s payment export was affected. Several other customers experienced adjacent but distinct symptoms, so mentions of a company name in the notes should not be treated as proof that its export had the same problem.\n\nOpening review. Bao thanked everyone for joining and said the team should be especially careful not to conflate the delayed dashboard refresh with a missing settlement export. The dashboard refresh was late for a number of workspaces after a cache invalidation change. Separately, a connector retry caused some scheduled exports to be delivered late. A third issue affected an optional notification banner. The incident commander had created separate tracking items for each symptom, although people occasionally used “the payments incident” as shorthand in conversation. Participants were asked to identify the specific service and customer before drawing conclusions.\n\nCustomer evidence, collected from support and verified against job records:\n\n• Tidewater Freight reported that its daily reconciliation dashboard showed older figures than expected. The dashboard refreshed successfully after the cache was cleared. Its scheduled CSV export was generated and delivered at the normal time. Support confirmed this from the delivery record and the customer confirmed receipt. The customer did not report a missing payment or a failed transfer.\n\n• Kestrel Supply opened a ticket saying that its export “was not there when our team looked.” The wording initially led the support agent to select the missing-export category. The export job record, however, showed a completed delivery to Kestrel’s configured endpoint at 08:12, before the customer’s usual review. The customer later clarified that the file had been present, but the team had looked in a different folder. The customer’s dashboard also displayed an earlier refresh time; that was the separate cache issue. No export-delivery failure was found for Kestrel.\n\n• Bluefin Dental’s export was delayed by a connector retry. It eventually arrived, and the customer’s account did not have a failed or missing export. Its initial message asked whether the delay meant a transfer had been reversed. Payments operations checked the transfer ledger and found no reversal. The customer was describing uncertainty, not asserting that funds had gone missing.\n\n• A Meridian Analytics test workspace had a deliberately disabled export destination. The test was documented as a staging check and was not an affected customer account. A screenshot from that workspace appeared in the incident channel and was briefly mistaken for evidence about a production export. The screenshot’s header plainly said “sandbox.”\n\nDiscussion of the customer that matters for the export-delivery question. The group reviewed Tidewater Freight specifically because its dashboard complaint was repeated in several messages and appeared next to the general incident label. The delivery record showed its CSV export completed at the expected time; support had separately confirmed receipt. The only verified Tidewater Freight symptom was stale dashboard data, which was repaired by refreshing the cache. Bao asked the reliability engineer to confirm that the export status was not being inferred from the dashboard status. The engineer confirmed the two pipelines had separate health checks and that Tidewater’s export check was green throughout the reported window.\n\nThe room also considered whether the word “reconciliation” implied an export issue. The support lead said it did not: Tidewater used the dashboard for reconciliation and had never stated that the CSV was missing. A customer’s workflow can depend on a dashboard without that dashboard’s refresh status determining the outcome of a separate export job. The team agreed that a correct incident summary should say “dashboard refresh delayed for Tidewater Freight; scheduled export delivered normally,” rather than “Tidewater’s payment export was delayed.”\n\nOther evidence discussed. One account manager forwarded a message from Kestrel Supply that said, “We could not find the file at first.” The forwarded message was quoted in a longer thread whose subject was “export delivery.” The account manager warned that subject lines were not reliable evidence of the underlying issue. In the follow-up, Kestrel stated, “We found it in the endpoint folder; it had been delivered before we checked.” That clarification was linked to the delivery record and closed the missing-file report.\n\nBluefin Dental’s connector retry generated a warning in the application log, but the warning represented a delayed attempt, not a failed final delivery. The export completed after retry. The customer’s phrase “our morning file was late” was accurate; it should not be generalized to Tidewater Freight, whose corresponding job completed on schedule. The engineer cautioned against using the presence of one customer’s retry warning as a reason to mark every customer as affected.\n\nAn unrelated integration test used a sample account with a name resembling a customer workspace. The test generated a notification with the words “payment export unavailable.” The notification was generated in a sandbox and was not a production event. The incident coordinator asked that it remain in the appendix for traceability but not be included in the customer-impact count. No participant disputed that instruction.\n\nRoot-cause discussion. The dashboard delay followed a cache invalidation change. The export retry behavior followed a separate connector condition. The banner issue followed a front-end deployment. The team decided not to combine the causes into a single claim merely because all three were discussed during the same meeting. Engineering’s tests showed the dashboard pipeline could recover while an export remained unaffected, and the reverse was also possible. The incident records would therefore retain separate service names and customer impact fields.\n\nAction items. Bao will update the incident summary with the verified customer-by-customer facts. The support lead will correct Kestrel Supply’s ticket category from “missing export” to “customer could not initially locate delivered file.” The reliability engineer will attach Tidewater Freight’s successful delivery record to the dashboard ticket so that the two symptoms are not conflated. Account management will explain to Bluefin Dental that its delayed export completed after a retry and that no transfer reversal was found. The staging screenshot will remain labeled as sandbox evidence.\n\nClosing note. The meeting did not establish that every customer mentioned in the incident channel had an export problem. It did establish that Tidewater Freight’s dashboard was stale for a period and that its scheduled CSV export was delivered normally. Anyone preparing a customer-facing summary should preserve that distinction, use the customer-specific evidence, and avoid treating nearby references to another customer’s delayed export as evidence about Tidewater’s export. The attendees thanked one another for taking care with the wording and agreed that accuracy was preferable to a simpler but misleading summary.",
