    "state": "Tidewater Freight — manager status update\nFrom: Beatriz Petrov, operations lead\nTo: payments operations manager\nSubject: Kindly review: connector retry incident and account impact\n\nGood afternoon. I am sending a deliberately detailed status update so that the customer-impact record remains accurate even where several accounts used similar language. The incident room was busy, and the phrase “the export issue” was used for more than one symptom. I have separated each account’s verified facts below. Please regard the account-specific support confirmation and service records as stronger evidence than the general incident label or a nearby example from another account.\n\nExecutive summary. The connector service retried some scheduled jobs after a destination response was slow. Some jobs completed after retry, while other jobs were unaffected. There was also an unrelated dashboard cache delay and a staging test involving a disabled destination. Those details can make the incident sound broader than it was. We should not infer that a particular customer had a failed export simply because the same update mentions an export failure elsewhere.\n\nAccount review — Kestrel Supply. Kestrel’s original ticket was titled “Missing payment export,” and the first message said, “It wasn’t where our analyst expected it.” The title was selected before the operations check and should not be treated as a verified failure classification. The job record confirms that Kestrel’s scheduled file was delivered to the configured endpoint at 08:12. In its reply, Kestrel explained that its analyst initially checked a local folder rather than the endpoint folder. The analyst later located the file there. Support therefore closed the report as a location misunderstanding, not a missing delivery. Kestrel’s dashboard also showed a stale refresh timestamp, but that was tracked under a separate cache item.\n\nAccount review — Bluefin Dental. Bluefin’s scheduled export encountered a co