    "state": "Kestrel Supply — application and server log review, with operator annotations\nPrepared by Rosa Tanaka, reliability operations. This extract combines the application job stream, support-side observations, and the follow-up review. It is intentionally not a complete raw dump: routine heartbeat entries have been omitted, while nearby customer records are retained to show why a customer name must be matched to its own job. The incident label was shared by several workspaces, but each line’s workspace field identifies the record to which it belongs.\n\nReview note. The service generates a queued event, one or more attempt events, and a final delivery status. A retry warning is not equivalent to a failed job. The final status and destination receipt determine whether the file was delivered. Dashboard refresh events are emitted by a separate component. They can appear close in time to export events, but they do not determine export status. The following excerpt has been arranged in chronological order for readability.\n\n07:55:11 dashboard-cache INFO workspace=Tidewater Freight action=refresh-start reason=scheduled\n07:55:13 dashboard-cache WARN workspace=Tidewater Freight action=stale-key-found component=overview\n07:55:18 export-worker INFO workspace=Tidewater Freight job=TF-447 state=queued\n07:55:41 export-worker INFO workspace=Tidewater Freight job=TF-447 state=delivered destination_receipt=present\n07:56:02 dashboard-cache INFO workspace=Tidewater Freight action=refresh-complete\n\n08:02:09 export-worker INFO workspace=Kestrel Supply job=KS-882 state=queued\n08:02:12 export-worker INFO workspace=Kestrel Supply job=KS-882 attempt=1 destination=configured_endpoint\n08:02:15 export-worker INFO workspace=Kestrel Supply job=KS-882 state=delivered destination_receipt=present\n08:02:18 support-console NOTE workspace=Kestrel Supply ticket-title=\"Missing payment export\"\n08:05:34 support-console NOTE workspace=Kestrel Supply customer-message=\"We didn't see it in the folder we checked.\"\n08:09:47 support-console NOTE workspace=Kestrel Supply agent-request=\"Please confirm which folder was checked.\"\n08:12:03 support-console NOTE workspace=Kestrel Supply customer-follow-up=\"We found it in the endpoint folder; it had arrived before we looked there.\"\n\n08:14:50 export-worker INFO workspace=Bluefin Dental job=BD-190 state=queued\n08:15:01 export-worker WARN workspace=Bluefin Dental job=BD-190 attempt=1 response=slow\n08:15:03 export-worker INFO workspace=Bluefin Dental job=BD-190 action=retry-scheduled\n08:16:42 export-worker INFO workspace=Bluefin Dental job=BD-190 attempt=2\n08:16:48 export-worker INFO workspace=Bluefin Dental job=BD-190 state=delivered destination_receipt=present\n08:18:07 payments-ledger INFO workspace=Bluefin Dental transfer_check=reversal-found result=no\n\n08:26:10 export-worker INFO workspace=Meridian Analytics test_workspace=staging job=MA-T17 state=queued\n08:26:14 export-worker WARN workspace=Meridian Analytics test_workspace=staging destination=disabled\n08:26:16 export-worker INFO workspace=Meridian Analytics test_workspace=staging job=MA-T17 state=test-complete\n\nThe records immediately above are the principal evidence for this review. The first customer’s dashboard cache warning and the nearby export completion belong to Tidewater Freight. Kestrel Supply’s job has its own queued event, a first attempt, and a final delivered state with a destination receipt. The Kestrel support ticket’s title was generated before the job review. Its later customer response clarifies that the file had been delivered and that the initial search was in a different folder. Bluefin Dental’s retry warning and later completion are separate job events under Bluefin’s workspace. The staging entry is a test and does not describe a production account.\n\n08:41:22 support-console NOTE workspace=Kestrel Supply issue-category=missing-file status=under-review\n08:43:10 support-console NOTE workspace=Kestrel Supply internal-comment=\"Could be same as slow connector report? Check account-level receipt before changing status.\"\n08:45:55 export-worker INFO workspace=Kestrel Supply job=KS-882 receipt-query=success\n08:48:31 support-console NOTE workspace=Kestrel Supply agent-comment=\"Endpoint receipt predates customer's first search; no second delivery attempt needed.\"\n\n09:03:02 export-worker INFO workspace=Bluefin Dental job=BD-191 state=queued\n09:04:40 export-worker WARN workspace=Bluefin Dental job=BD-191 attempt=1 response=slow\n09:07:26 export-worker INFO workspace=Bluefin Dental job=BD-191 state=delivered destination_receipt=present\n09:11:08 payments-ledger INFO workspace=Bluefin Dental transfer_check=reversal-found result=no\n\n09:19:54 dashboard-cache INFO workspace=Kestrel Supply action=refresh-start reason=scheduled\n09:20:08 dashboard-cache WARN workspace=Kestrel Supply action=refresh-late component=overview\n09:21:46 dashboard-cache INFO workspace=Kestrel Supply action=refresh-complete\n09:24:14 support-console NOTE workspace=Kestrel Supply customer-comment=\"Dashboard was old when I opened it, but the export is now in the endpoint folder.\"\n\nAn operator initially placed the 09:20 dashboard warning beside Kestrel’s “missing file” ticket in a draft timeline. The placement was chronological, not causal. The dashboard component produced its own late-refresh event, while the export job had already recorded successful delivery. A shared account name does not make the two components’ statuses interchangeable. The customer’s own comment distinguishes them: the dashboard was old, and the export was in the endpoint folder.\n\n09:39:02 incident-room NOTE author=operations summary-draft=\"Kestrel may have had a delayed export; Bluefin definitely saw retry behavior.\"\n09:43:17 incident-room NOTE author=reliability correction=\"Please check job KS-882; final state is delivered and receipt is present. The delayed retry belongs to BD-190/BD-191.\"\n09:51:03 incident-room NOTE author=support confirmation=\"Kestrel located file in endpoint folder; category should describe initial search, not delivery failure.\"\n10:02:26 incident-room NOTE author=incident-coordinator=\"Separate account-level impacts in final record; retain staging lines only as test context.\"\n\nThe review also checked whether there was any evidence of a transfer reversal for Kestrel. The ledger query in the excerpt is for Bluefin Dental, where the customer asked about a reversal after its delayed file. No Kestrel reversal event appears in the reviewed payment records. The ticket concerned file location, not a reported transfer loss. The absence of a reversal record was not used as the main reason for the export classification; the decisive evidence is the successful Kestrel job and endpoint receipt.\n\nThe diagnostic logger emitted several generic warnings during this period. Some warn lines mean a slow response, some mean a stale cache key, and some identify a test destination. The operations runbook instructs reviewers to read the component, workspace, and final state together. A phrase such as “warning” without those fields is not sufficient to label an account as affected. Similarly, the shared term “incident” is a grouping label for investigation, not an event emitted by the export worker.\n\nAt handoff, the reliability operator asked the next shift to retain the raw customer-specific lines and avoid copying a neighboring workspace’s symptoms. The ticket should state that Kestrel’s file was delivered to its configured endpoint before the customer searched there. The dashboard refresh was late, but it belongs to a different service event. Bluefin had the connector retry; Tidewater had a stale dashboard while its export delivered normally; Meridian’s disabled destination was only a staging test. The incident can be described accurately without assigning every nearby warning to every account.\n\nFinal operator annotation. Kestrel Supply’s export job KS-882 reached the delivered state and has a destination receipt. The initial support title and the dashboard warning are not stronger than that account-specific evidence. The separate Bluefin retry is retained in this excerpt because it was a plausible source of confusion during triage, not because it changes Kestrel’s result. The review is closed with Kestrel’s delivery marked successful and its initial location confusion documented separately.",
