    "state": "Larkspur Labs course-office amendment packet, copied into my notes because the original binder got soaked during the open-house tea incident. I am Dalia Castillo, registrar, and I keep telling myself this is a tidy packet, although the pages have three different staple patterns and someone has written “FINAL-ish” on the cover. The original agreement with Northbank Preparatory says the evening robotics course has a standard capacity of 24 students and a materials allowance of $18 per enrolled student. The college pays the instructor a fixed $1,200 for the term; that is not part of the per-student allowance, even though it sits in the same paragraph and tends to confuse people. The signed amendment dated August 2 changes the capacity to 28 students, effective immediately, and leaves the materials allowance unchanged. A later signed amendment dated August 19 says: “For the September cohort only, actual materials reimbursement is limited to $17 per student, notwithstanding the earlier $18 figure. All other financial provisions remain in force.” So, yes, both amounts appear in the packet, and no, they do not both apply to September.\n\nThe enrollment sheet for the September cohort lists 26 students as active on the first class date. Two students withdrew before class began and are marked inactive in the margin, so the 26 already excludes them. One student joined during week two and is marked “late add, attending,” which makes 27 actual students for the month if the question is about attendance rather than the first-day sheet. The course administrator’s reimbursement worksheet uses first-class-date enrollment, as specified in section 4, not monthly attendance. I mention this because the late-add student has a perfectly good campus card and a box of parts, but the worksheet does not count that student in the reimbursement base.\n\nThe instructor’s receipt bundle totals $463.00. One receipt for $19.00 is for a personal soldering iron, with a note from the instructor saying “I’ll keep this for home”; it is expressly excluded by the agreement. The remaining receipts total $444.00 and are eligible materials purchases. Under the September amendment, the allowable ceiling is calculated by multiplying the 26 first-day students by $17 each. That calculation produces $442. The instructor has therefore submitted eligible expenses that are $2 above the reimbursement ceiling; the school does not reimburse the excess. Somebody penciled “26 x 18 = 468” beside the worksheet, which would have been the earlier rate, but the signed September amendment supersedes that rate for this cohort.\n\nThere is also a separate room-charge clause, because apparently one short agreement was not enough. The school may charge $35 per evening when the class uses the fabrication room after 6 p.m. There were four such evenings, but the second one was a scheduled safety orientation that the amendment explicitly says is not billable. The room log lists five late entries, yet one is a duplicate scan for the same Thursday. The finance assistant has resolved the log: three billable evenings, not four or five. Thus the room charge is $105. It is not deducted from the materials reimbursement; the agreement treats it as a separate invoice line. A margin note says “minus room = $337?” but that combines two different obligations and is not how the contract works.\n\nIn the email thread attached to the packet, the instructor asks whether the school can pay the full $444 in receipts and sort out the difference later. The registrar replies that the cap controls, and the instructor answers “Got it, I will use the $442 ceiling.” No one approved a waiver. The packet also includes a quote for next term: 30 students, $20 each, and a $1,350 instructor fee. It is clearly labelled “planning only, not an amendment,” and applies to the winter term, not this September cohort. The number 30 is therefore not the September capacity, and the $20 rate is not applicable here. I am wri