    "state": "Incident postmortem — Saltmarsh Rentals CI pipeline, 14 June\n\nWith apologies for the inconvenience, here is our careful account of the build interruption. At 09:12 the Linux runner pool began rejecting jobs after a routine image refresh. The dashboard showed a red deployment badge, but the production service remained available throughout; the release team had not started deployment. Several people kindly checked the same alert, which was helpful but did not change the underlying cause.\n\nThe immediate cause was a runner image that lacked the pinned version of the `jq` utility. The build script attempted to parse a configuration file and exited with code 127. The image refresh also changed a cache directory, but our review found no evidence that the cache change caused this incident. A separate staging job failed later because of an expired test token; that failure was unrelated and should not be counted as part of the runner outage.\n\nAt 09:38 Sven Haddad noticed the missing utility in the job log. At 09:51 he restored the pinned package to the image definition, and at 10:04 the runner pool passed its smoke tests. The queued builds then completed normally. No customer data was lost, no production rollback occurred, and no release was cancelled. We are grateful that colleagues waited for the verification rather than retrying the deployment repeatedly.\n\nFor follow-up, we have opened two actions: add a package-presence check to the image smoke test, and document the runner-image pinning process for the on-call team. Both actions are assigned to the platform group for the next maintenance cycle. We have not yet scheduled that cycle, so please do not treat either action as completed. The incident review is now closed, with sincere thanks to everyone who supplied logs and timelines.",
