    "state": "Saltmarsh Health — event policy page, revision 6.2, posted beside the registration spreadsheet\nA charmingly simple policy, provided nobody reads just the bold headings. The page applies to the Lantern Hall symposium only. “Net eligible registration receipts” means settled attendee charges for the symposium, less attendee refunds already issued. Exclude taxes, sponsor pledges, catering payments, staff registrations, and unsettled authorizations. A venue credit is calculated only if net eligible receipts are at least $1,200. The credit is 10% of the amount above $1,200, capped at $90. Round the final credit to the nearest whole dollar, with 50 cents rounded upward. This is a credit against the next venue invoice, not cash owed to the organizer.\n\nPolicy note: if the net is below $1,200, credit is zero. Do not use gross receipts. The event administrator may approve a room extension separately, but the extension does not alter this formula. The page’s sidebar still says “up to $90”; that is not a promise that every event receives $90, but apparently the sidebar has tenure.\n\nSpreadsheet excerpt, copied at 16:20, Tuesday:\n\nRow 1 — Standard attendee, settled: 18 registrations × $75 = $1,350.\nRow 2 — Student attendee, settled: 6 registrations × $50 = $300.\nRow 3 — Attendee refund issued yesterday: 2 × $50 = $100. These were originally included in row 2’s gross registrations; subtract them once.\nRow 4 — Sales tax collected: $124. This is held for remittance and is not an eligible receipt.\nRow 5 — Sponsor pledge, “Blue Lantern Fund”: $500. Pledge has not been paid and is not an attendee charge.\nRow 6 — Catering add-on payments, settled: $240. The catering vendor is paid directly; this amount is not a registration receipt.\nRow 7 — Staff check-in badges, paid by department card: 4 × $75 = $300. The policy excludes staff registrations even if a department card has settled.\nRow 8 — Authorizations pending settlement: 3 × $75 = $225. The payment screen ca