e same check-in event after a network timeout. The duplicate has the same event UUID as the 07:41 entry and must not create another crew or another arrival time.\n07:52: vendor memo: “Our standard call is 30 minutes early and four people, so $120 should appear.” The memo is a generic estimate, not the event-specific condition. Even under its own multiplication, 4 × $30 is $120 only if the timing trigger applies.\n08:00: booked access opens. 08:03: organizer scans in, roster role organizer; not crew. 08:06: venue staff mark doors ready. This does not move the access time.\n\n08:18, billing preview: Early access line, $120; parking line, $12; total preview $132. The preview was generated from the stale rehearsal schedule that said 07:40 and the scanner’s equipment count. It is not a final bill. The current booking sheet still says 08:00 access and four contracted crew. The line item title does not prove that the rule is met.\n\n08:25, operations correction: “For today, crew checked in 07:41, which is 19 minutes before 08:00. The 07:39 scanner hit is equipment only; GPS and plan times do not control.” The access surcharge rule says more than 20 minutes early. No early-access surcharge is due. Parking remains due at $12 for the single van.\n\n08:29, another automated note: “The 07:38 route plan is 22 minutes early, so charge $120.” The arithmetic would be 08:00 minus 07:38 = 22 minutes, but the plan is not the arrival event specified by policy. 08:31, Farid: “Wonderful, a bill based on a van that had not checked in.” 08:40, payment status: no surcharge posted; parking fee settled. Audit closes with no other crew check-ins and no additional vehicles.",
