rately. A and B attended it; C is booked; D attended it last month; E has not attended. Because B is a contractor, the rule says this orientation is optional for B, but that does not make the required confidentiality and safety items optional. D is not a contractor. These distinctions have caused confusion before, so please keep status notes specific rather than writing simply “onboarding done.”\n\nThe files have several near-matches. Starter A’s acknowledgement has a signature and date. B’s form has a date but no signature. C’s file has a signature on the emergency contact page, not the confidentiality acknowledgement. D’s safety record shows the previous surname but the correct employee number; the record was entered by the trainer and has a timestamp. E’s supervisor forwarded a generic slide deck, but there is no attendance record for the required briefing. A message from the hiring coordinator says, “they’re all good, let them in,” but it does not attach evidence and is not an approval under the rule.\n\nI recommend enabling accounts only where both documented requirements are met. This is a checklist status, not a judgment about anyone’s conduct. If you approve, I’ll add a note that D’s name mismatch was resolved by identifier and preserve the audit trail. I have not changed the enabled status for A or D. The open actions are to collect B’s briefing record, get C’s signed acknowledgement, and schedule E for both requirements. Please confirm whether you want a separate report for optional equipment orientation; it is not part of the account-enablement decision.",
